      "textEn": "145\n\nThis is a curiosity, O Lord, of Brahma. The father's desire for his son's death is what is being used. 46. Meaning of the word\n\nKim ut—quite less; Anuvashan—obedient and complete sons; Sadhun—supreme devotee; Tadrushan—of that kind; Guru—gods;\nDevatan—gods; Etat—this; Kauthulam—doubt; Brahman—Oh Brahmin; Asmakam—our;\nVidham—remove; Prabho—Oh Lord; Pitah—father; Putrayah—for son; Yat—that; Dvesha—hatred; Eerjya—jealousy; Maranaya—to kill;\nPrayojitah—applied..\n\nMaharaj Yudhishthir further asked: How was it possible for a father to be so aggressive towards his obedient, virtuous, and respectful son? Hey\nBrahmin, hey Swami, I have never heard such a paradox that a loving father, with the intention of killing his virtuous son, punishes him. Please remove my doubts regarding this matter.\nPlease do.\nMeaning: In the history of human society, rarely has any affectionate father been found to punish his virtuous and devoted son. Therefore, Maharaj Yudhishthir wanted to remove this doubt from Narad Muni.\nHe wanted to. Thus, under the seventh canto of Srimad Bhagavat, \"The terror of Hiranyakashipu in the universe\"\nThe Bhakti Vedanta Tatparya of the fourth chapter named \"The terror of Hiranyakashipu in the universe\" is completed. Chapter five.\n\nHiranyakashipu's virtuous son, Prahlad Maharaj.\n\nPrahlad Maharaj was unable to follow his guru's orders because he was always engaged in the worship of Lord Vishnu. As described in this chapter, Hiranyakashipu tried to kill Prahlad Maharaj\nby stabbing him with a snake and by putting him under the feet of an elephant, but still he\nremained unsuccessful. Hiranyakashipu's guru Shukracharya had two sons named Shand and Amark, who were assigned the responsibility of teaching Prahlad\nMaharaj.\nAlthough the teacher tried to teach the boy Prahlad about politics, economics,\nand other worldly subjects, but the boy did not"
