      "textEn": "151\n\nThe mind runs outside with desires. \"He who has unwavering devotion to Krishna, constantly manifests all the virtues of Krishna and the gods. But in which there is no devotion to God, virtues are not found,\nbecause he remains busy in the material world through mental imaginations, which is the external form of God.\" (Bhagavat 5.18.12) The so-called educated philosophers and scientists who attain an intellectual level\nare unable to differentiate between truth, that is, eternal, and untruth, that is, transient. Vedic order is always there.\nJyotirgam—Every person should abandon the perishable level and attain the eternal level. The soul\nis eternal, and stories about the eternal soul are true knowledge. It is said elsewhere—Apashyatam\nAtmatatvam griheshu grihamedhinam—Those who remain connected to the body-consciousness and those who, at the level of material sensory pleasures,\nremain attached to life as householders, cannot see the welfare of the eternal soul.\nPrahlad\nThe Maharaj confirmed this by saying that the person who desires success in life should immediately understand from the right source what his selfishness is in and in spiritual consciousness, how he\nshould mold his life. A person should understand that they are a part of Krishna and\nbe completely dedicated to his lotus feet for definite spiritual success. In this physical world,\nevery person, due to egoistic intellect, struggles through births and births with difficult life struggles. Therefore,\nPrahlada Maharaj has recommended that to stop this continuous cycle of birth and death,\nman should go to the forest. In the Varna system, a man first becomes a celibate, then a householder, then a forest dweller, and finally\na renunciant. One means to go to the forest, to accept the life of Vanaprastha, which is the life between household life and renunciation.\nAs confirmed in the Vishnu Purana (3.8.9)—By accepting the practice of Varna and Ashrama, a man—\nis worshiped—By 